/*Q2. Develop a Java application to demonstrate constructor overloading using a class named Test.
Class: Test
Create a class named Test with the following specifications:
Instance Variables:
• private int i — stores the first integer value.
• private int j — stores the second integer value.
Constructors:
• Test() — initializes both i and j to 0.
• Test(int i) — initializes i with the given value and j to 0.
• Test(int i, int j) — initializes both instance variables with the given values.
Method:
• void display() — displays the values of i and j on separate lines in the following format:
Value of i: <i>
Value of j: <j>
The Main class should perform the following operations in order:
1. Create an object named obj! using the default constructor and display its values.
2. Read an integer and create an object named obj2 using the single-parameter constructor. Display its values.
3. Read two integers and create an object named 0b73 using the two-parameter constructor. Display its values.
Input Format:
• The first line contains an integer for 0bj2's instance variable 7.
• The second line contains two space-separated integers for 0b73's instance variables 7 and 7.
Output Format:
Display the values of and 7 for each object, in the following order:
• First, display the values for obj1.
• Next, display the values for 0bj72.
• Finally, display the values for 0b73.
For each object, display
Value of i: <i>
Value of j: <j>
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Test {
    private int i;
    private int j;
 
    Test() {
        i = 0;
        j = 0;
    }
 
    Test(int i) {
        this.i = i;
        this.j = 0;
    }
 
    Test(int i, int j) {
        this.i = i;
        this.j = j;
    }
 
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Value of i: " + i);
        System.out.println("Value of j: " + j);
    }
}
 
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        Test obj1 = new Test();
        obj1.display();
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        Test obj2 = new Test(a);
        obj2.display();
        int x = sc.nextInt();
        int y = sc.nextInt();
        Test obj3 = new Test(x, y);
        obj3.display();
        sc.close();
    }
}
